  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level low pressure continues to spin over far southern\nOntario, with associated troughing in place over the eastern\nGreat Lakes and the Upper Ohio Valley. Two main vorticity \nmaxima rotate through the flow into tonight - one closer to the \nLake Erie shoreline this afternoon and a more vigorous one \ndropping across our CWA this evening. These will be responsible \nfor continuing the chances for scattered to numerous, but brief,\nshowers and localized thunderstorms through 06Z or so tonight. \nCoverage appears best with the second wave, with most activity \nnorth of US-422. Still, thunderstorms will become a bit less \nlikely with that wave with diurnal stabilization.\n\nChances of strong to severe thunderstorms this afternoon/evening \nremain quite low overall. While surface-based CAPE may peak in \nthe 1000-1200 J/kg range this afternoon, particularly north of \nI-80, drier air above 700mb should limit updraft depth, and \n0-6km shear of less than 30 knots will be a detriment to storm \norganization. Along with downdraft CAPE values of 500 J/kg or \nless, this suggests that the damaging wind threat is very low. \nHail may be observed with a few of the more vigorous cells, but \nthe above parameters point to diameters remaining below severe \nlimits.\n\nPrecipitation coverage drops off after 06Z with the departure of\nthe second shortwave, but a few isolated showers may continue\nnorth of I-80 with continued NW flow off of Lake Erie.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLingering cool advection within northwest flow results in\nhigh temperatures around 10 degrees below the daily average\nMonday while high pressure supports dry weather (save for low\nprobability showers north of I-80).\n\nThe upper low lifts toward Quebec by Tuesday, while surface high\npressure overspreads the region. This continues dry weather\nthrough Wednesday, while the rising 500mb heights point to\ntemperatures rising back to close to seasonable levels.\n\nThe next trough is still pegged by ensembles to arrive Thursday.\nTiming issues that far out mean that the hazard potential is \ndifficult to ascertain currently. Long- range machine-learning \nguidance does show a slight uptick in severe weather potential, \nbut this risk will need more time and model cycles to be \nproperly evaluated.",
